meta {
  name: Entity Ownership Extraction
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

post {
  url: {{aiUwLambdaUrl}}/workflows/entity-ownership-extraction/run
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "loan_id": {{loan_id}}
  }
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Read-only layered entity ownership (workflows PR #44). Reads the loan's
  entity documents from POS, traces the ownership down to the ultimate
  individual owners (UBOs), flags the required guarantors per RBI's per-level
  >=20% rule, and RETURNS the whole answer. Nothing is persisted.

  Routing:
    Bruno -> Function URL -> ai-uw-dispatcher -> ai-uw-borrower-eligibility

  Registered as `entity-ownership-extraction` in the dispatcher's
  WORKFLOW_ROUTING and in the borrower-eligibility handler REGISTRY. Excluded
  from that handler's `run-all`.

  ── Read-only twin of `Entity Ownership Tracing` ────────────────────────────

  Same tracing logic — the tree code is imported from entity_ownership_tracer,
  not re-implemented — with every AI-UW backend dependency removed:

                              Tracing              Extraction (this one)
    body                      loan_id +            loan_id
                              validation_id
    BorrowerEligibility-      required (looked     not involved
      Validation                up when omitted)
    EntityOwner rows          written              none
    summary PATCH            yes                  no
    phase callback           yes                  no
    idempotent               no (writes per run)  yes (changes nothing)

  Use THIS one to read a loan's structure (spot checks, Retool panels, prompt
  tuning). Use `Entity Ownership Tracing` when Phase 1 needs the tree
  persisted against a validation record.

  Because there is no validation_id, the fallback that makes Tracing risky —
  "take the latest validation for the loan", which silently attaches a run's
  output to a PREVIOUS validation while still returning 200 — does not exist
  here. There is nothing to attach to.

  ── Body ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    loan_id          required — the only field you need
    entity_name      optional — overrides the name POS reports, for a loan POS
                     spells differently from its ownership documents
    model            optional — overrides the extraction model
    loan_documents   optional — skips the POS document fetch (fixtures)

  Missing loan_id -> {"status": "error", "error": "loan_id is required"}

  ── Where the inputs come from ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  Both reads go straight to POS (no load_loan_context, so the backend's
  WorkflowLoanSummary is never fetched).

  Borrower entity name, in order of precedence:
    1. `entity_name` in this body
    2. GET /api/v1/loan-applications/{loan_id}       -> result.formData.entity_name
    3. GET /api/v1/loan-application-form/{loan_id}/  -> result.formData.entity_name

  Whichever answered comes back as `borrower_entity_name_source`
  (input | pos_loan_application | pos_loan_application_form). If none does:
  {"status": "error", "error": "borrower entity_name not found in loan application"}

  Documents: GET /api/v1/loan-application-documents/{loan_id}, filtered to the
  slugs operatingAgreementByLawsStockCertPartnershipAgreement,
  articlesOfOrganizationIncorporation and einLetter. Deleted files dropped.

  ── Timing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  One OCR + AI extraction per entity document, 6 in parallel. A layered loan
  carries several Operating Agreements, so expect tens of seconds — `timeout`
  is 0 (no limit) for exactly this reason. Unlike Tracing there are no
  persisted rows to read while you wait: the response body is the only output,
  so let the request finish.

  ── Response fields worth checking ─────────────────────────────────────────

    status                       PASS, or REVIEW when the borrower doc is
                                 missing or a high-severity condition fired
    borrower_entity              the name that was traced
    borrower_entity_name_source  which of the three sources supplied it
    ownership_tree               borrower at depth 0, children nested
    required_guarantors          individuals reached via a fully-qualifying
                                 >=20% chain
    ultimate_individual_owners   leaf individuals (UBOs)
    layered_structure_detected   true when any direct owner of the borrower is
                                 itself an entity
    conditions                   missing docs, foreign-entity due diligence,
                                 low-confidence matches, manual review
    validation_checklist         per-entity ownership sums, documents present
    entity_documents_found       POS files matched by slug
    entity_records_extracted     of those, how many the AI could read
    persisted                    always false — stated so its absence is not
                                 read as a failed write
    parameters                   the thresholds this answer was computed under
                                 (model, guarantor_threshold, max_depth, slugs)

  A document that fails to extract is skipped: the entity it described ends up
  with missing_document: true, a `missing_ownership_document` condition fires
  and status becomes REVIEW. Compare entity_documents_found against
  entity_records_extracted to spot it.

  Full contract: docs/ENTITY_OWNERSHIP_EXTRACTION.md in ai-underwriting-workflows.
}
